# The live / grounding-capable scorer image.
#
# WHAT THIS CAN DO THAT THE BATCH IMAGE CANNOT. The batch `Dockerfile` states of
# itself that it "CANNOT BUILD A SUBSTRATE AND CANNOT SCORE RAW TEXT" — it
# hydrates every prompt from content-addressed refs in a frozen substrate. This
# image is the complement: it installs gilda and indra, so it can construct a
# `ScoringRecord` from a raw INDRA Statement, which is what
# `ScoringRecord.__post_init__` calling `ScoringRecord.resolve_entities`
# unconditionally makes mandatory on the live path. That, and only that, is the
# difference. Everything else — the kernel, the parser, the verdict grid — is
# the same code.
#
# THIS IS A SCORER CLI, NOT A SERVER. `research/serving_deployment.md` §1
# records that no serving process exists in this repository: no HTTP entry
# point, no `main` that listens. Its §5 sketches one; nothing in it is built.
# So there is no `serve` verb here and the image name must not imply one. The
# entry point is `python -m indra_belief.scorers.scorer`, the same CLI a
# developer runs from a checkout.
#
# IMPORT TRAP, RESOLVED — kept as a note because serving_deployment.md §5.1
# still describes the hazard. `src/indra_belief/scorers/scorer.py` used to
# re-export the DECOMPOSED architecture at module import while its own `--arch`
# flag defaulted to `monolithic`, so importing names from that module silently
# got you the non-default architecture. The decomposed architecture has been
# removed: `scorer.py` now re-exports the monolithic scorer, there is no `--arch`
# flag, and the two paths cannot disagree because there is only one.
#
# WHY THIS INVERTS THE BATCH FILE'S INSTALL. The batch image installs its deps
# explicitly and finishes with `pip install --no-deps .`, because pyproject.toml
# declares gilda and indra as HARD requirements and a plain `pip install .`
# would pull in exactly what that profile exists to omit. Here the same fact
# points the other way: gilda and indra are precisely what this profile needs,
# so a plain `pip install .` is the correct install and `--no-deps` would be
# wrong. The batch file's three documented additions still apply and are carried
# over verbatim in intent:
#   pandas   — undeclared anywhere in pyproject.toml and not referenced in
#              `src/` (grep finds zero hits), but MEASURABLY loaded: importing
#              `indra_belief.comparison.cli` leaves `pandas` in `sys.modules`.
#   openai / anthropic — declared, but only in the OPTIONAL `[llm]` extra, so a
#              plain install omits them. `model_client.py` imports `openai` for
#              the Bedrock mantle endpoint and `anthropic` for a sibling
#              transport.
#   scipy    — arrives transitively via scikit-learn; pinned explicitly so the
#              floor is visible rather than inherited.
#
# BUILD TRAP, reproduced on this host before this file was written. On
# linux/arm64 `python:3.13-slim`, `pip install gilda indra` FAILS:
#
#     gcc -fno-strict-overflow ... -c src/adeft/score/_score.c -o ...
#     error: command 'gcc' failed: No such file or directory
#     ERROR: Failed building wheel for adeft
#
# `adeft` is a hard gilda dependency. Be precise about why, because the tempting
# summary is wrong: it is NOT that adeft ships no wheel — adeft 0.4.0 publishes a
# `py3-none-any` one. It is that pip resolves this closure to adeft 0.13.0, which
# is distributed only as an sdist (`Downloading adeft-0.13.0.tar.gz`), and whose
# `adeft.score._score` Cython extension must therefore be COMPILED at install
# time. The fix is `build-essential` in the BUILDER stage ONLY; the multi-stage
# copy keeps the toolchain out of the runtime image, and the runtime-stage import
# check below proves nothing built in the builder needs it at run time. With it,
# the same install reports `Successfully built adeft pysolr`.
#
# MEASURED SIZE, from `docker image inspect` on the image this file built,
# against the batch image's 535 MB (`indra-belief:batch` = 534,805,452 B).
# Re-confirmed byte-for-byte on the rebuild that added `/app/data` to the
# mkdir+chown below — that layer is 0 B in `docker history`, since it changes
# directory metadata only:
#   * this image  993,002,546 B = 993 MB          (1.86x the batch image)
#   * site-packages, in-container `du`  911.5 MB. In `docker history` it is a
#     single 848 MB COPY, and no OTHER layer this file adds exceeds 2 MB (the
#     next largest is scripts/ at 1.7 MB). The rest is the python:3.13-slim
#     base, whose own layers are 100 MB + 38.8 MB + 3.9 MB.
#   * indra installed  190.4 MB, of which indra/resources is 143.5 MB
#   * gilda installed  2.7 MB
# The +458 MB over the batch image is PACKAGES, not their data: `find / -name
# 'grounding_terms*' -o -name bio_ontology.pkl` returns nothing in this image.
# The ~36 MB of gilda terms and the ~470 MB ontology pickle are the volume's
# job — see the resources section below — so the whole grounding capability
# costs less in the image than the ontology cache alone would.

ARG PYTHON_VERSION=3.13

FROM python:${PYTHON_VERSION}-slim AS builder

ENV PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1 \
    PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1

# Builder only. gilda's `adeft` dependency compiles a C extension from sdist on
# this platform; without a compiler the build dies as quoted in the header.
# Nothing here reaches the runtime stage.
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends build-essential \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

WORKDIR /build

# Wheels first, in their own layer: the slow, rarely-invalidated step. Versions
# track pyproject.toml's floors, plus the four additions the header explains.
# --timeout/--retries because a files.pythonhosted.org read timeout was observed
# while probing this dependency set.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --timeout 120 --retries 10 \
        "gilda>=1.1.0" \
        "indra>=1.22.0" \
        "numpy>=1.24.0" \
        "scipy>=1.10.0" \
        "scikit-learn>=1.2.0" \
        "pandas>=2.0.0" \
        "openai>=1.0.0" \
        "anthropic>=0.18.0"

COPY pyproject.toml README.md ./
COPY src/ ./src/

# WITH deps, unlike the batch file: pyproject.toml's hard gilda/indra
# requirements are the point of this profile, and the layer above has already
# satisfied them, so this resolves to a no-op install of the package itself.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --timeout 120 --retries 10 .


# The gate runs AT BUILD TIME, not in the shipped image — same discipline as the
# batch file, aimed at the complementary claim.
#
# The batch gate proves an ABSENCE (the batch suites pass with neither package
# installed). This one proves a PRESENCE, and the two suites are chosen to be
# exact complements: the batch file excludes
# `tests/test_prepared_execution_parity.py` by name and says the parity it
# asserts "is covered by the full suite on a grounding-capable environment,
# which is where it belongs." This is that environment, so this is where that
# suite runs.
#
# WHAT THIS GATE DOES NOT PROVE — stated plainly, because it is the easy wrong
# inference. `tests/test_prepared_execution_parity.py` monkeypatches grounding
# OFF: its record fixture replaces `ScoringRecord.resolve_entities` with a no-op
# for the duration of the test. So it proves the live PRODUCER runs correctly
# with indra installed and agrees call-for-call with the batch producer. It does
# NOT prove this image can ground anything. The suite that proves grounding is
# `tests/test_grounding_collision.py` — it `pytest.importorskip`s gilda and
# calls the real `GroundedEntity.resolve` on labelled collision cases — and it
# needs the mounted resources, which by design are not in any layer here. It
# therefore belongs to a runtime verify path against a warmed volume, not to a
# build gate. Do not read this stage as grounding coverage.
#
# Three cases are EXPECTED to skip: the `requires_substrate` ones gate on
# `data/comparison/grounding_replay/manifest.json`, and `.dockerignore` excludes
# `data/` from the build context on purpose. Locally, with the substrate
# present, the same file is 16 passed.
FROM builder AS test

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir "pytest>=7.0"
COPY tests/ ./tests/
RUN python -m pytest -q --no-header -p no:cacheprovider \
        tests/test_prepared_execution_parity.py \
    && python -c "import gilda, adeft, indra.statements, indra.ontology.bio; print('live profile: grounding packages present')" \
    && touch /build/.live-profile-verified


FROM python:${PYTHON_VERSION}-slim AS runtime

ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

# Non-root, same uid as the batch image so a shared host volume has one owner.
RUN useradd --create-home --uid 10001 belief

COPY --from=builder /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages

WORKDIR /app
COPY --chown=belief:belief scripts/ ./scripts/
COPY --chown=belief:belief pyproject.toml ./

# A C extension compiled in the builder against a shared library that is absent
# from the slim runtime would otherwise surface on the first request. Fail the
# BUILD instead. (Imports only — `gilda.ground()` is deliberately not called
# here; it would download and index resources into a layer, which is the one
# thing this image must not do.)
#
# ITS POSITION AFTER `WORKDIR /app` IS LOAD-BEARING, and this is not stylistic:
# the first build of this file put the check before it and the build FAILED,
# with `ImportError: Blocked import of pydoc from current working directory for
# security reasons` raised out of `import gilda`. nltk — pulled in by adeft,
# pulled in by gilda — installs an import finder in `nltk/inisec.py` that
# resolves each spec's origin and calls `Path(origin).resolve().relative_to(cwd)`
# to decide whether a module is being loaded out of the working directory. When
# the working directory is `/`, that test is true of EVERY path on the system,
# so the stdlib's own `pydoc` is misread as a cwd import and refused. Measured:
# cwd `/` fails, cwd `/build` succeeds, and the `PYTHONSAFEPATH=1` the error
# message recommends does NOT help, because the finder compares resolved paths
# and never consults `sys.path`. The real image is unaffected — ENTRYPOINT and
# HEALTHCHECK both inherit `WORKDIR /app` — but running this image with `-w /`
# would break gilda, so do not.
RUN python -c "import gilda, adeft, indra.statements"

# Forces the `test` stage to build. Without this line Docker would skip it,
# since nothing else depends on it, and the gate above would be decorative.
COPY --from=test /build/.live-profile-verified /app/.live-profile-verified

# RESOURCES LIVE ON A VOLUME, NEVER IN A LAYER. Both relocations are verified
# against the installed packages, not assumed:
#   * gilda resolves its resource directory as `pystow.join('gilda', __version__)`
#     in `gilda.resources`, and pystow honours PYSTOW_HOME.
#   * `indra.ontology.bio.ontology.CACHE_DIR` is built from
#     `get_config('INDRA_RESOURCES')`, and `indra.config.get_config` reads
#     `os.environ` BEFORE the config file.
# Measured on this host, this is what stays out of the image: gilda 1.6.1's
# resource dir is a single file, `grounding_terms.tsv.gz` at 38,060,066 B, from
# the public `gilda` S3 bucket via `botocore.UNSIGNED` in
# `gilda.resources._download_from_s3` (no credential); `get_gilda_models` pulls
# a further ~10.5 MB `gilda_models.json.gz` lazily. indra's ontology cache,
# `bio_ontology.pkl`, is 492,535,810 B.
ENV PYSTOW_HOME=/app/resources/pystow \
    INDRA_RESOURCES=/app/resources/indra

# chown BEFORE `USER belief` and before BOTH VOLUME declarations, so a fresh
# named or anonymous volume is seeded from a directory the runtime user can
# already write. `/app/data` is covered here too, and that is a fix, not tidying:
# before this line covered it, `/app/data` existed only as the root-owned
# directory Docker creates for the VOLUME declaration — measured `drwxr-xr-x 2
# root root` in an image that runs as `uid=10001(belief)` — so `docker run --rm
# -v /app/data ...`, the anonymous-volume case and any fresh named volume, was
# seeded unwritable and the CLI's first ledger append would fail.
#
# WARNING: a BIND mount does not inherit this chown; the host directory's own
# ownership governs and the image's chown never applies to it. [M] Measured here
# on Docker Desktop, a bind of a host directory appears in-container as
# `belief belief` and IS writable, because its virtiofs layer remaps ownership —
# so the failure does not reproduce on this host. [R] On a Linux host, where bind
# ownership passes through unremapped, a host directory not owned by uid 10001 is
# expected to be unwritable and the operator must chown it. That expectation was
# not run here. It is also not a contract docker-compose.yml already carries: it
# declares `./data:/app/data` and a `:ro` variant and says nothing about
# ownership or uid.
RUN mkdir -p /app/data /app/resources/pystow /app/resources/indra \
    && chown -R belief:belief /app/data /app/resources

# The corpus, exactly as in the batch image: the CLI resolves inputs relative to
# the working directory, and it is tens of GB and gitignored, so it is mounted,
# never baked.
VOLUME ["/app/data"]
# The grounding resources: ~36 MB gilda terms plus a ~470 MB ontology pickle,
# neither of which belongs in a layer.
VOLUME ["/app/resources"]

USER belief

# Fails fast and offline if the image is wrong, without touching data/ or a
# provider: the grounding packages must import, and both resource roots must
# exist. It deliberately does NOT call `gilda.ground()`. serving_deployment.md
# §3.4 measures the first ground at 10.9-19.7 s and ~2 GB RSS, and the first
# FPLX lookup at 7.5 s and ~7.5 GB RSS — a healthcheck that did that would blow
# the timeout, thrash the page cache, and race the warm-up it is supposed to be
# observing. The imports alone cost a median 2.2 s in this image (n=9
# in-container runs of `import gilda, indra.statements`, range 1.7-2.6 s), so the
# 10 s timeout carries ~4x headroom over the slowest sample observed; that margin
# is the reason the timeout is left at the batch file's value rather than raised.
# An earlier single-sample 3.30 s reading is superseded: quoted to three
# significant figures it did not reproduce, and the headroom argument never
# depended on the precision.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=10s --start-period=5s --retries=3 \
    CMD python -c 'import os,sys,gilda,indra.statements; sys.exit(0 if os.path.isdir(os.environ["PYSTOW_HOME"]) and os.path.isdir(os.environ["INDRA_RESOURCES"]) else 1)' || exit 1

ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "indra_belief.scorers.scorer"]
CMD ["--help"]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPERATOR NOTES — invocation, and the warm-up this image REQUIRES.
#
# Not added to docker-compose.yml in this change. That file is the operator
# surface for the batch image and its contract is correct as written; the
# compose entry for this profile is the operator's to land.
#
#   docker build -f Dockerfile.live -t indra-belief:live .
#
#   # First run, ONCE, with a NAMED volume — populates it in-container.
#   docker volume create indra-belief-resources
#   docker run --rm -v indra-belief-resources:/app/resources \
#     --entrypoint python indra-belief:live -c \
#     "import gilda; gilda.ground('BRAF'); \
#      from indra.ontology.bio import bio_ontology; \
#      bio_ontology.initialize(); bio_ontology.get_children('FPLX','JUN_family')"
#
#   # Thereafter, warm:
#   docker run --rm -v indra-belief-resources:/app/resources \
#     -v "$PWD/data:/app/data" indra-belief:live --help
#
# WHY THE WARM-UP IS AN OPERATOR STEP AND NOT A BUILD STEP.
# `indra.ontology.bio.ontology.BioOntology.initialize` has NO prebuilt-pickle
# download path — read it: it either loads CACHE_FILE or calls `self._build()`
# over indra's 144 MB of bundled resources plus network ("this may take a few
# minutes", its own log line), then pickles itself to CACHE_FILE. So the pickle
# is CONSTRUCTED, and constructing it during the build would bake ~470 MB into a
# layer. Building it IN-CONTAINER also sidesteps the cross-platform and
# cross-Python-version unpickle question that copying a host pickle would raise.
#
# WARNING: a plain `docker run` with no `-v` gets an ANONYMOUS volume, which is
# discarded per container. That silently re-downloads gilda's terms and REBUILDS
# the ontology on every single run. Use a named volume or a chowned bind mount.
#
# Steady state after warm-up, from serving_deployment.md §3.4: ~116 ms per
# record on a cold LRU, 0.0137 ms warm. The caches are `functools.lru_cache` in
# `src/indra_belief/data/entity.py` and therefore process-local, so a worker in
# this image is meaningfully stateful and should be long-lived.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
